CAT = "ukcareAwareness"; SEG = "AWR"
# Standard 9 Awareness of mental health and dementia, Standard 16 Awareness of learning disability and autism
Q = [
("Which is the most common type of dementia in the UK?",
 "Alzheimer's disease", ["Vascular dementia", "Frontotemporal dementia", "Dementia with Lewy bodies"],
 "Alzheimer's disease is the most common cause of dementia. Vascular dementia, caused by reduced blood flow to the brain, is the second most common."),
("Which of these can be an early sign of dementia?",
 "Forgetting recent events", ["A sudden rash on the arms", "Sharper short-term memory", "A strong appetite"],
 "Early signs include problems with recent memory, finding words, planning, orientation and changes in mood. Report any changes you notice."),
("How does delirium usually differ from dementia?",
 "It starts suddenly", ["It develops slowly over many years", "It only affects younger adults", "It never causes any confusion"],
 "Delirium is a sudden change in confusion or alertness, often caused by infection, dehydration or medicines. It needs urgent reporting because the cause can often be treated."),
("A person with dementia keeps asking to go home to see her mother, who died years ago. What is the best approach?",
 "Respond to her feelings", ["Remind her firmly that her mother died", "Ignore the question each time", "Tell her she is going home later"],
 "Repeating painful news can cause fresh grief. Acknowledge the feelings behind the words, talk about her mother and offer comfort and distraction."),
("What does \"reasonable adjustments\" mean?",
 "Changes that remove barriers", ["Rules that apply to everyone the same", "Cuts to care hours when budgets are tight", "Changes staff make for their own ease"],
 "Under the Equality Act 2010 services must make reasonable adjustments so disabled people, including those with dementia, a learning disability or autism, are not put at a disadvantage."),
("Which of these is a common mental health condition?",
 "Anxiety", ["Arthritis", "Asthma", "Diabetes"],
 "Anxiety and depression are among the most common mental health conditions. Others include bipolar disorder and schizophrenia."),
("Which of these could be a sign of depression?",
 "Losing interest in hobbies", ["Talking more than usual", "Wanting more visitors each week", "Sleeping the usual amount"],
 "Signs include low mood, loss of interest, tiredness, changes in sleep and appetite, and withdrawal. Report changes so the person can get support."),
("Which law allows some people to be assessed and treated in hospital for a mental disorder without their agreement?",
 "The Mental Health Act 1983", ["The Mental Capacity Act 2005", "The Care Act 2014", "The Equality Act 2010"],
 "The Mental Health Act 1983 sets out when people can be detained, often called being sectioned, for assessment and treatment of a mental disorder."),
("Why is early diagnosis of dementia helpful?",
 "It helps people plan ahead", ["It cures the condition", "It stops all the symptoms", "It means care is not needed"],
 "There is no cure, but early diagnosis gives access to support and some treatments and lets people plan, for example by making a lasting power of attorney."),
("How can the environment help a person living with dementia?",
 "Clear signs and good lighting", ["Busy patterned carpets", "Frequent changes of room", "Mirrors along every corridor"],
 "Good lighting, clear signage, contrasting colours and familiar objects help orientation. Busy patterns and reflections can confuse or distress."),
("What is a learning disability?",
 "A lifelong reduced ability to learn", ["A short illness that passes in time", "A mental health condition", "A reading difficulty such as dyslexia"],
 "A learning disability affects the way a person understands information, learns new skills and copes independently. It starts before adulthood and is lifelong. Dyslexia is a learning difficulty, not a learning disability."),
("What is autism?",
 "A lifelong developmental difference", ["An illness that can be cured", "A condition caused by parenting", "A type of learning disability"],
 "Autism is a lifelong developmental difference affecting how people communicate, interact and process the world. Some autistic people also have a learning disability, but many do not."),
("Which of these is a common sensory difference for autistic people?",
 "Finding noise overwhelming", ["Needing louder music to relax", "Having no sense of touch", "Seeing only in black and white"],
 "Many autistic people are over- or under-sensitive to sounds, lights, smells, touch or tastes. Reducing sensory overload can prevent distress."),
("What is the Oliver McGowan Mandatory Training?",
 "Learning disability and autism training", ["Moving and handling training", "Fire safety and evacuation training", "Food hygiene and allergy training"],
 "The Health and Care Act 2022 requires CQC-registered providers to make sure staff get learning disability and autism training suited to their role. The Oliver McGowan Mandatory Training is the standardised training the government recommends for this."),
("An autistic man becomes distressed when his daily routine changes. What helps most?",
 "Explaining changes in advance", ["Changing his routine more often", "Not telling him until it happens", "Asking him to just cope with it"],
 "Predictability matters to many autistic people. Give advance warning of changes, use clear language or visual aids and follow his care plan."),
("What does STOMP aim to do?",
 "Stop over-medication", ["Speed up medicine rounds", "Stop online medicine sales", "Store medicines more cheaply"],
 "STOMP stands for stopping over-medication of people with a learning disability, autism or both, especially psychotropic medicines used to manage behaviour."),
("What is diagnostic overshadowing?",
 "Blaming symptoms on a disability", ["Getting a second medical opinion", "Taking a long time to diagnose", "Diagnosing two conditions at once"],
 "Diagnostic overshadowing happens when new symptoms are wrongly put down to a learning disability, autism or mental illness, so real health problems are missed."),
("What is a hospital passport?",
 "A guide to the person's needs", ["An ID card for entering wards", "A form for booking appointments", "A record of hospital bills"],
 "A hospital passport gives staff key information about a person with a learning disability or autism: how they communicate, their needs, likes and dislikes."),
("From what age can people on their GP's learning disability register have a free annual health check in England?",
 "14", ["18", "25", "65"],
 "People aged 14 and over on the GP learning disability register are entitled to an annual health check, which helps spot health problems early."),
("How should you communicate with a person with a learning disability?",
 "Use simple words and check", ["Speak quickly to keep their interest", "Use technical terms for accuracy", "Talk to their carer, not them"],
 "Use clear, simple language, one idea at a time, visual aids where helpful and check the person has understood. Speak to the person directly."),
("Which statement about autism is accurate?",
 "Every autistic person is different", ["All autistic people avoid eye contact", "Autistic people cannot feel empathy", "Autism only affects children"],
 "Autism is a spectrum and affects people in very different ways. Generalisations lead to poor support; get to know the individual."),
("What is the social model of disability?",
 "Barriers in society disable people", ["A person's condition is the problem", "Disability is a medical illness", "Disabled people need curing"],
 "The social model says people are disabled by barriers in attitudes, buildings and systems, not by their impairment, so removing barriers is the focus."),
("A person with a mental health condition is talking about ending their life. What should you do?",
 "Stay with them and get help", ["Change the subject to cheer them", "Promise to keep it a secret", "Wait to see if they mean it"],
 "Take any talk of suicide seriously. Stay with the person, listen and get urgent help from a senior or emergency services as your agreed ways of working say."),
("Why can a person with dementia become distressed in the late afternoon or evening?",
 "Tiredness and fading light", ["Their medicines always run out", "They dislike every evening meal", "Visitors are never allowed then"],
 "Increased confusion and distress later in the day, sometimes called sundowning, can be linked to tiredness, changing light, hunger or unmet needs."),
]
